<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · movement</p><h1 class="enc-title">Eurocommunism</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1970s</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Eurocommunism was a trend among West European communist parties in the 1970s and 1980s, led by the Italian party under Enrico Berlinguer and the Spanish party under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago">Santiago</a> Carrillo. Eurocommunists claimed independence from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> and accepted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parliamentary-democracy">parliamentary democracy</a> and civil liberties. The Soviet suppression of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague-spring">Prague Spring</a> in 1968 did much to inspire the movement.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Eurocommunism was a trend within several Western European communist parties, prominent in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and to a lesser extent in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, whose members said they had developed a theory and practice of social transformation more relevant to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-europe">Western Europe</a> than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marxism-leninism">Marxism-Leninism</a>. The origin of the term was much debated in the mid-1970s. According to Perry Anderson its main theoretical foundation was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-gramsci">Antonio Gramsci</a>'s writing, which questioned the sectarianism of the left and encouraged communist parties to seek social alliances.</p><p>The crushing of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague-spring">Prague Spring</a> in 1968 was a turning point. The Italian Communist Party and the Communist Party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> firmly denounced the occupation, and the French party criticised a Soviet action publicly for the first time in its history. Détente also played a part: with war less likely, Western communists felt less pressure to follow Soviet orthodoxy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Italian and Spanish parties adopted Eurocommunism most enthusiastically, and the Finnish party was dominated by Eurocommunists, while most of the French party stayed aligned with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, apart from a brief turn in the mid-to-late 1970s. In 1976 Enrico Berlinguer of the Italian party spoke in Moscow of a pluralistic system and of a socialism possible only in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>.</p><p>His Historic Compromise with the Christian Democrats ended with the kidnapping and murder of Aldo Moro in 1978. Santiago Carrillo of the Spanish party wrote Eurocomunismo y estado in 1977 and took part in drafting the democratic constitution as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> emerged from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-franco">Francisco Franco</a>. In 1977 Berlinguer, Carrillo and Georges Marchais of the French party met in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a> and set out the new way.</p><p>The parties embraced public-sector middle-class workers and new movements such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feminism">feminism</a> and gay liberation, and the idea spread to parties outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, including those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> dissolved many parties split. Some became social democrats, as the Italians did in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-party-uganda">Democratic Party</a> of the Left, while others moved into green politics or kept some communist positions.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Critics argued that the Eurocommunists did not break decisively with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, the Italian party taking that step only in 1981 after the repression of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solidarity-polish-trade-union">Solidarność</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, and that they lacked a clear strategy and identity, claiming to differ from both Soviet communism and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/social-democracy">social democracy</a> while resembling one or the other. The Trotskyist Ernest Mandel saw them as nationalist movements that had abandoned internationalism, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enver-hoxha">Enver Hoxha</a> called Eurocommunism anti-communism. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikhail-gorbachev">Mikhail Gorbachev</a> named it in his memoirs as an influence on glasnost and perestroika.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Eurocommunism” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Prague Spring of 1968, Alexander Dubček's attempt at 'socialism with a human face', and its suppression shaped reform communism and <b>Eurocommunism</b> across Europe, and Jan Palach's self-immolation in January 1969 became an emblem of protest.</blockquote><a href="/empire/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In Western Europe communist parties joined post-war governments and in the 1970s developed <b>Eurocommunism</b>, and since 1957 communists have often won power in the Indian state of Kerala.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/communism">Communism · Spread &amp; Variants</a></li></ul></section>
